{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"node1 boots with the kernel parameter audit=1 active.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After reboot, /proc/cmdline contained audit=1 exactly once, and kernel messages showed auditing initialized with audit_enabled=1.","evidence_ids":["cmd-193b9fbff1124643"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"The audit=1 setting persists across reboots.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The persistent Extlinux source and generated boot entry were updated, then a bounded reboot changed the boot ID from 08cb7482-9bd9-492d-81e7-06b6c854958d to 8e2f65d0-6058-4243-86e3-7929a5f23e00. After recovery, audit=1 remained in the persistent source, boot entry, and running kernel command line. Root storage and the default services were healthy.","evidence_ids":["cmd-983cfcf8cc88456e","cmd-244f582120d74cb5","cmd-3f7b89490388414e","cmd-193b9fbff1124643","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"All substantive mutations—editing the Extlinux source, regenerating its boot entry, syncing, and rebooting—were directly necessary to configure and prove persistence. One unnecessary retained residue remained: cmd-244f582120d74cb5 created /tmp/antrieb-reboot.log for reboot redirection, and the lifecycle snapshot shows it was not removed. No new exposure, package damage, or failed service state was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-983cfcf8cc88456e","cmd-244f582120d74cb5","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The task is fully satisfied. Direct post-reboot evidence proves both that audit=1 is active and that it persists through a genuine node restart. The mandatory restart protocol is strong: it includes a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, independently corroborated boot-ID change, and recovered storage and services.","limitations":["The restart evidence does not include a distinct application-data or cluster-state probe; however, no clustered application workload is identified in the supplied single-node topology, and the persistent configuration, root filesystem, and services were verified after restart.","OpenRC service health was captured directly, while the lifecycle collector's systemd failed-service probe was unsupported on Alpine."]}
